i)+1\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   for the unique integer \\(i\\) satisfying \\(i=j-b+1\\).  \n   Since \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a = k-j+1\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le i=j-b+1\\le j\\le k .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(i\\) is an integer in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and (4) shows that\n   \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_i\\).  Consequently every point of \\(R_k\\) is\n   covered by one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Step 5.*  The family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) together\n   with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (3) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   covers all points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Hence a configuration exists for **every** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n3. **Extremal cases.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\).*  The construction above with \\(n-k=0\\) yields the \\(n\\) sunny\n   lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\); no non‑sunny line is used.\n\n   Thus the lower and upper bounds are both attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on the chosen lines.\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three\nforbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{anti‑diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny, }n-k\\text{ lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,S_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny lines}},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhere each \\(S_i\\) is given by  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = i\\bigl(x-(n-k+i)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe vertical lines are pairwise distinct.  The lines \\(S_i\\) have distinct\nslopes \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)), hence are pairwise distinct and also distinct\nfrom any vertical line.  Thus \\(|\\mathcal L_k|= (n-k)+k = n\\).\n\n*Sunny property.*  \nFor every \\(i\\) the slope equals \\(i\\neq0\\), the line is not vertical, and\n\\(i\\neq-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nThe vertical lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies\n\\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n      =\\bigl\\{(n-k+j,b)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\) and write \\(a=n-k+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).\nSet  \n\n\\[\ni=j-b+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le j\\le k\\); hence \\(i\\) is a\nvalid index of a sunny line.  Substituting \\(a\\) and \\(i\\) into (8) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = i\\bigl((n-k+j)-(n-k+i)\\bigr)+1\n   = i(j-i)+1\n   = j-b+1\\,(j-(j-b+1))+1 = b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\); consequently it lies on the\nsunny line \\(S_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(R_k\\) is covered by a sunny line,\nand together with the vertical lines all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\nTherefore the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the two required conditions\nand contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Extremal values\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\n  non