sfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\qquad\\text{(all integers }k\\in[0,n]\\text{ except }k=2\\text{).}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular remainder.**  \n   *Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.*  \n   – Every non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or anti‑diagonal.  \n   – In each column \\(x=c\\) a horizontal or anti‑diagonal line can cover at most one point, whereas a vertical line can cover the whole column.  \n   – By replacing each horizontal or anti‑diagonal line with a suitable vertical line we never decrease the set of points covered by the non‑sunny lines.  \n   – Hence we may assume that the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n   – After removing the points of these columns, the uncovered points form the right‑angled triangular set  \n     \\[\n     R_k=\\bigl\\{(m+j,b)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\bigr\\}.\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n     This set contains exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points.\n\n2. **Covering the triangle with sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma.* The triangle \\(R_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(k\\neq2\\).  \n\n   – **Construction for \\(k=0\\).** No point remains; take only the vertical lines (1).  \n   – **Construction for \\(k=1\\).** The single point \\((m+1,1)\\) is covered by any sunny line, e.g. the line through \\((m+1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n   – **Construction for \\(k\\ge3\\).** For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n     \\[\n     S_i:\\; y=i\\bigl(x-(m+i)\\bigr)+1 .\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n     The slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.  \n     For a point \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\) write \\(a=m+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) and set \\(i=j-b+1\\).  \n     The inequalities defining \\(R_k\\) guarantee \\(1\\le i\\le j\\le k\\); substituting \\(a\\) and \\(i\\) into (3) yields \\(b\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n     Consequently the family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covers all of \\(R_k\\).  \n\n   – **Impossibility for \\(k=2\\).**  \n     The triangle \\(R_2\\) consists of the three points  \n     \\[\n     (m+1,1),\\;(m+1,2),\\;(m+2,1).\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n     Any line through two of these points is vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non‑sunny.  \n     Hence a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, so at least three sunny lines would be required – contradicting the hypothesis \\(k=2\\).  \n\n3. **Putting everything together.**  \n   The family  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   contains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines for every admissible \\(k\\) (i.e. all \\(k\\neq2\\)).  \n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only value of \\(k\\) that cannot be realised is \\(k=2\\); all other integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on the chosen lines.\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden\ndirections  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{anti‑diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to a triangular remainder  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\) and contain\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach non‑sunny line is of one of the three types in (7).\n\n*Vertical* lines cover an entire column \\(\\{x=c\\}\\) of \\(T_n\\); column \\(c\\)\ncontains \\(n+1-c\\) points.  \n*Horizontal* and *anti‑diagonal* lines intersect a given column in at most one\npoint, because for a fixed \\(x\\) the equations \\(y=\\text{const}\\) and\n\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) determine a unique \\(y\\).\n\nConsequently, in any column the total number of points that can be covered\nby the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines is at most the number of those lines that are\nvertical.  Replacing every horizontal or anti‑diagonal line by a vertical\nline (choosing a column not already used) never decreases the set of points\ncovered by the non‑sunny lines.  Hence we may assume, without loss of\ngenerality, that the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAfter removing the points of these columns, the uncovered points are exactly\n\n\\[\nR_k=\\bigl\\{(m+j,b)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(R_k\\) is a right‑angled triangular array of size \\(k\\); it\ncontains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points.\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following one:\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) (with \\(k\\le n\\)),\n> cover the triangle \\(R_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Covering the triangle  \n\nWe treat the possible values of \\(k\\) separately.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \n\\(R_0\\) is empty; the family (9) already covers all of \\(T_n\\).  No sunny\nline is used.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \n\\(R_1=\\{(m+1,1)\\}\\).  Any sunny line passing through this point suffices;\nfor instance the line through \\((m+1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope\n\\(\\frac{1}{1-m}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge3\\).*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=i\\bigl(x-(m+i)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each\n\\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\).  Write \\(a=m+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) and set  \n\n\\[\ni=j-b+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(R_k\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\), which yields\n\\(1\\le i\\le j\\le k\\); thus \\(i\\) is a valid index.  Substituting \\(a\\) and\n\\(i\\) into (11) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=i\\bigl((m+j)-(m+i)\\bigr)+1=i(j-i)+1=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\) and lies on that sunny\nline.  Therefore every point of \\(R_k\\) belongs to one of the lines\n\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\), and the family \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^k\\) covers the whole\ntriangle.\n\n*Case \\(k=2\\).*  \nHere  \n\n\\[\nR_2=\\{(m+1,1),\\;(m+1,2),\\;(m+2,1)\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of the three points is either vertical,\nhorizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non‑sunny.\nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most one of the points in (13).\nTo cover the three points we would need at least three sunny lines, which\ncontradicts the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.  Hence **\\(k=2\\) is\nimpossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the original problem  \n\nFor every admissible \\(k\\neq2\\) we combine the vertical lines (9) with the\nsunny lines constructed above:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\},\n\\qquad m=n-k .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, covers all\npoints of \\(T_n\\) (t