e anti‑diagonal\n    covers the points \\((n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\); the line \\(L_{1}\\) covers\n    the remaining point \\((n-2,2)\\).  The extra sunny line \\(L_{2}\\) is\n    redundant but guarantees that exactly two sunny lines are present.\n    For \\(n=3\\) a direct construction (vertical line \\(x=1\\) together with\n    two sunny lines through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\)) works, so the case\n    \\(k=2\\) is settled for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4.  **The general case \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n    Put \\(m=n-k\\) and use the vertical lines  \n\n    \\[\n    x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m . \\tag{3}\n    \\]\n\n    After removing the points of these columns the uncovered set is the\n    right‑angled triangular array  \n\n    \\[\n    R_k=\\bigl\\{(m+j,b)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\bigr\\},\n    \\tag{4}\n    \\]\n    which contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points.\n\n    For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n    \\[\n    S_i:\\; y=i\\bigl(x-(m+i)\\bigr)+1\n    \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n    \\]\n\n    The lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct and sunny.  \n    Let \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\); write \\(a=m+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) and set  \n\n    \\[\n    i=j-b+1 .\n    \\tag{6}\n    \\]\n\n    Because \\(1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le j\\le k\\); substituting\n    \\(a\\) and \\(i\\) into (5) gives \\(b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n    \\(S_i\\).  Consequently the family \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) covers the whole\n    triangle \\(R_k\\).\n\n    The complete set of lines  \n\n    \\[\n    \\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\},\n    \\qquad m=n-k,\n    \\tag{7}\n    \\]\n\n    consists of \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and\n    contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5.  Combining the constructions of steps 3 and 4 we obtain, for **every**\n    integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\n    satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having precisely \\(k\\)\n    sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a given integer \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\) put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines whose union\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The cases \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  They are all non‑sunny and each column of \\(T_n\\) is completely covered,\n  so the required family exists.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  (non‑sunny) together with the sunny line through the points\n  \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its equation is  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,x+\\frac{n}{n-1},\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  whose slope \\(\\frac{1}{1-n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n  The vertical lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\), which\n  lies on the sunny line.  Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny\n  line exists.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  – If \\(n=3\\) we may take the non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) and the two sunny\n    lines  \n\n    \\[\n    y=\\tfrac12x\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y=x,\n    \\]\n\n    which together cover the three points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n  – For \\(n\\ge4\\) consider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n    \\[\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\text{non‑sunny: }x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\quad\\text{and}\\quad 