-\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a given integer \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\) put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines whose union\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The cases \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  They are all non‑sunny and each column of \\(T_n\\) is completely covered,\n  so the required family exists.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  (non‑sunny) together with the sunny line through the points\n  \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its equation is  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,x+\\frac{n}{n-1},\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  whose slope \\(\\frac{1}{1-n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n  The vertical lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\), which\n  lies on the sunny line.  Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny\n  line exists.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  – If \\(n=3\\) we may take the non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) and the two sunny\n    lines  \n\n    \\[\n    y=\\tfrac12x\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y=x,\n    \\]\n\n    which together cover the three points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n  – For \\(n\\ge4\\) consider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n    \\[\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\text{non‑sunny: }x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\quad\\text{and}\\quad x+y=n+1,\\\\[1mm]\n    &\\text{sunny: }L_1:\\;y=2x-(2n-4)\\quad\\text{(passes through }(n-2,2)),\\\\\n    &\\qquad\\qquad\\;L_2:\\;y=2x\\quad\\text{(any sunny line)}.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\tag{6}\n    \\]\n\n    The vertical lines cover all columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-3\\); the anti‑diagonal\n    \\(x+y=n+1\\) covers the points \\((n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\); the line\n    \\(L_{1}\\) covers the remaining point \\((n-2,2)\\).  The extra sunny line\n    \\(L_{2}\\) is redundant but guarantees that exactly two sunny lines are\n    present.  Thus a configuration with precisely two sunny lines exists\n    for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(m=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le m\\le n-3\\)).  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).  The points that are still uncovered form\nthe right‑angled triangular set  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\bigl\\{(m+j,b)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points.\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=i\\bigl(x-(m+i)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), every\n\\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\).  Write \\(a=m+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)\nand set  \n\n\\[\ni=j-b+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le j\\le k\\); therefore the\nindex \\(i\\) is admissible.  Substituting \\(a=m+j\\) and this value of \\(i\\)\ninto (9) yields\n\n\\[\ny=i\\bigl((m+j)-(m+i)\\bigr)+1=i(j-i)+1=b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_i\\).  Consequently every point of\n\\(R_k\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\},\n\\qquad m=n-k,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\),\nand has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of the set \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
