1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(m+(k-1)=n-1\\) distinct lines.  To reach the required total of\n\\(n\\) lines we add any sunny line that does **not** intersect the triangle\n\\(R_k\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{extra}}:\\; y=kx+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{\\text{extra}}\\) is sunny (slope \\(k\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) and is distinct\nfrom all lines already listed.  The final family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal L_k\\cup\\{L_{\\text{extra}}\\}}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nand covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a\nrequired configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The exceptional case \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|T_3|=6\\) points:\n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nAssume a configuration with three distinct lines, exactly two of them sunny.\nSince at most one line can be non‑sunny, that line must be vertical,\nhorizontal or anti‑diagonal.  A direct inspection of the three possibilities\nshows:\n\n* **Vertical line \\(x=1\\).**  \n  The remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  Any sunny line that\n  contains both \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) would be horizontal (slope \\(0\\));\n  any sunny line that contains both \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) would have slope\n  \\(-1\\).  Hence two sunny lines cannot cover the three points.\n\n* **Horizontal line \\(y=1\\).**  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  Any line through\n  \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) is vertical; any line through \\((1,3)\\) and\n  \\((2,2)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  Again impossible.\n\n* **Anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=4\\).**  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  The only line through\n  \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  Hence impossible.\n\nTherefore no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for\n\\(n=3\\).  The values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable (the constructions of\nSections 1 and 2 work for \\(k=0,1,3\\) when \\(n=3\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are\n  attainable.\n\nHence the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
