            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n  are all non‑sunny and each column of \\(T_n\\) is completely covered; hence\n  a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  (non‑sunny) and the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\),\n\n  \\[\n  y=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,x+\\frac{n}{n-1},\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  whose slope \\(\\frac{1}{1-n}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n  The vertical lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\), which lies\n  on the sunny line.  Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line\n  exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  General construction for \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }0\\le m\\le n-2).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nUse the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not\nexceed \\(m\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The uncovered triangle  \n\nThe points that remain uncovered form  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\bigl\\{(m+j,b)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\na right‑angled triangular lattice of side length \\(k\\); \\(|R_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering \\(R_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=i\\bigl(x-(m+i)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\) can be written uniquely as \\(a=m+j\\) with\n\\(1\\le j\\le k\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le k-j+1\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni=j-b+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\le k-j+1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le j\\le k\\), and because\n\\(b\\ge1\\) we have \\(i\\le j-1\\le k-1\\).  Substituting \\(a=m+j\\) and this\n\\(i\\) into (8) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=i\\bigl((m+j)-(m+i)\\bigr)+1=i(j-i)+1=b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).  Consequently every point of\n\\(R_k\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\).\n\nThe diagonal points of \\(R_k\\) (those with \\(a-b=m\\)) correspond to\n\\(i=1\\); indeed \\(L_1\\) has equation \\(y=x-m\\) and passes through all\n\\((m+j,j)\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(m+(k-1)=n-1\\) distinct lines.  To reach the required total of\n\\(n\\) lines we add any sunny line that does **not** intersect the triangle\n\\(R_k\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{extra}}:\\; y=kx+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{\\text{extra}}\\) is sunny (slope \\(k\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) and is distinct\nfrom all lines already listed.  The final family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal L_k\\cup\\{L_{\\text{extra}}\\}}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nand covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a\nrequired configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The exceptional case \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|T_3|=6\\) points:\n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nAssume a configuration with three distinct lines, exactly two of them sunny.\nSince at most one line can be non‑sunny, that line must be vertical,\nhorizontal or anti‑diagonal.  A direct inspection of the three possibilities\nshows:\n\n* **Vertical line \\(x=1\\).**  \n  The remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  Any sunny line that\n  contains both \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) would be horizontal (slope \\(0\\));\n  any sunny line that contains both \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) would have slope\n  \\(-1\\).  Hence two sunny lines cannot cover the three points.\n\n* **Horizontal line \\(y=1\\).**  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  Any line through\n  \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) is vertical; any line through \\((1,3)\\) and\n  \\((2,2)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  Again impossible.\n\n* **Anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=4\\).**  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  The only line through\n  \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  Hence impossible.\n\nTherefore no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for\n\\(n=3\\).  The values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable (the constructions of\nSections 1 and 2 work for \\(k=0,1,3\\) when \\(n=3\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are\n  attainable.\n\nHence the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
