s of one of the three types:\n\n* vertical \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach of them belongs to \\(S_n\\) because \\((n-1)+1=n\\le n+1\\) and \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most one of the points in (1):\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most one of them (different \\(x\\)-coordinates);\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most one (different \\(y\\)-coordinates);\n* a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most one (different sums).\n\nHence the three points are covered **only** by sunny lines.  \nAny line through two of the points in (1) would be\n\n* \\(P_1P_2\\): vertical,\n* \\(P_1P_3\\): horizontal,\n* \\(P_2P_3\\): slope \\(-1\\),\n\nall of which are non‑sunny. Consequently a sunny line can pass through **at most one** of the three points.  \nWith exactly two sunny lines we can cover at most two of \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\); the third point stays uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\nThus **no** configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for all other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The cases \\(k=0,1,n\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\) for some \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); hence it lies on one of the lines. All lines are non‑sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add a sunny line