"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete construction for every admissible value of\u202f\\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny does **not** exist.\n\nThese two impossibility results are proved below.  \nThe existence of configurations for the remaining values of \\(k\\) (namely \\(k=0,1\\) and, when \\(n=3\\), also \\(k=3\\)) is also proved.  \nThe cases \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\) are left open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Choose the three points  \n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,n),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{3}=(2,n-1).\n   \\]\n   No two of them lie on a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one of them.  \n   A sunny line can also contain at most one of them, because the three points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)\u2011coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)\u2011coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\).  \n   Hence three distinct lines are needed to cover the three points, contradicting the hypothesis that only two sunny lines are available.\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Assume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   *Because no line is vertical, each line meets the first column \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, and the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots,(1,n)\\) must therefore lie on distinct lines.*  \n   Let the line that contains \\((1,i)\\) be denoted by \\(\\ell_i\\) \\((1\\le i\\le n)\\).  \n   The point \\((n,1)\\) has to belong to some \\(\\ell_t\\). The slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{1-t}{\\,n-1\\,}\\).  \n   For the line to be sunny we need this slope \\(\\neq0\\) and \\(\\neq-1\\); consequently \\(t\\neq1\\) and \\(t\\neq n\\). Thus \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \n\n   Consider now the second column \\(x=2\\), which contains the points \\((2,1),\\dots,(2,n-1)\\).  \n   Each sunny line meets this column in at most one point, so the \\(n\\) lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of the column; the column, however, contains \\(n-1\\) points, so at least one line does **not** meet the column inside the required set.  \n   The line \\(\\ell_t\\) that passes through \\((n,1)\\) meets the second column at the point\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(2,\\;t+\\tfrac{1-t}{\\,n-1\\,}\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   whose second coordinate is not an integer because \\(n\\ge4\\) makes the denominator \\(n-1\\ge3\\). Hence \\(\\ell_t\\) does not cover any point of the second column.  \n   Consequently the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the \\(n-1\\) points of the second column, each line covering exactly one of them. In particular the line \\(\\ell_1\\) (which contains \\((1,1)\\)) must meet the second column at \\((2,1)\\); this forces \\(\\ell_1\\) to be the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is **not** sunny \u2013 a contradiction.  \n\n   Therefore a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n3. **Existence for the easy values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). All are non\u2011sunny and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y = 2\\bigl(x-n\\bigr)+1 = 2x-2n+1 .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(2\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) and passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) that is not covered by the vertical lines; for every \\(a<n\\) the value of \\(y\\) given by \\(\\ell\\) is \\(\\le0\\), so no other required point lies on \\(\\ell\\). Hence exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines\n   \\[\n   L_{1}: y = x,\\qquad\n   L_{2}: y = -2x+5,\\qquad\n   L_{3}: y = 2x-3\n   \\]\n   have slopes \\(1,-2,2\\) (all admissible) and together cover the six points\n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\). Thus for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\nThese results settle the problem for the values \\(k=0,1\\) (for every \\(n\\ge3\\)) and for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\); they also show that \\(k=2\\) is never possible and that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nThe existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\) remains open."
